#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""msi-control-gui — Qt control centre for MSI laptops running Linux.

Front-end to msi-controld (which owns every EC write); this process is never
privileged and never touches sysfs directly.  All daemon traffic runs on one
worker thread so a slow EC transfer can never freeze the UI.

Sections (sidebar):
  Dashboard    live temps / fan speeds / RPM / battery
  Performance  shift mode (eco/comfort/sport/turbo) + cooler boost + MUX
  Fans         fan mode + draggable CPU/GPU fan curves (with factory ghost)
  Battery      charge limit (the "stop charging at N%" setting)
  Profiles     built-in + saved setting bundles
  Settings     daemon state, re-apply, factory capture, raw EC console

Design rules (same ones the daemon enforces — the GUI is just a client):
  * every value is validated by msi_control before it is sent;
  * nothing is written optimistically — the UI re-reads state after each
    command, so what you see is what the EC actually holds;
  * with the daemon down the GUI degrades to read-only instead of lying.

Theme: one token set per theme (light/dark), applied as a QPalette plus a
single application stylesheet, so every colour, radius and border comes from
one place and the two themes cannot drift apart.  `MSI_THEME=light|dark`
forces one; otherwise the desktop's own colour scheme decides, defaulting to
dark when the platform does not report one (as offscreen does not).

The look is MSI's: the accent is MSI red, and the app mark is a red shield
carrying the model name — the badge language a gaming laptop is expected to
have.  The temperature bands stay green→amber→orange→red, because those are
*status* rather than chrome, so the red accent never has to double as
"critical": an interactive control is red because it is the brand, a hot
reading is red because it is hot, and the two never mean the same thing.

Shape, spacing and type each come from one scale (`SPACE`, `R_CARD`/`R_CTRL`/
`R_INNER`, `TYPE_*`).  They used to be chosen per widget — six different corner
radii, and a mix of point- and pixel-sized fonts — which is the kind of drift
that makes an interface read as assembled rather than designed.

Font sizes are deliberately set in code, never in the stylesheet: a Qt
stylesheet `font-size` overrides QWidget.setFont(), which would silently
flatten the big readouts back to body size.
"""

